WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.544
<v Examiner>Exam case 6.

2
00:00:02.544 --> 00:00:03.394
<v Examiner>Diabetes.

3
00:00:03.844 --> 00:00:08.315
<v Examiner>Larry has recently been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus, an endocrine disorder,

4
00:00:08.315 --> 00:00:11.655
<v Examiner>after what seems to be many years of uncontrolled hyperglycaemia.

5
00:00:11.655 --> 00:00:15.664
<v Examiner>He must measure his blood sugar every day as he works with his diabetes nurse,

6
00:00:15.664 --> 00:00:18.542
<v Examiner>and he'll need insulin injections and other medications.

7
00:00:18.542 --> 00:00:22.190
<v Examiner>Larry is a little overwhelmed, and isn't sure what this insulin is for.

8
00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:25.634
<v Examiner>As his nurse, you need a robust understanding of what insulin does.

9
00:00:25.634 --> 00:00:28.203
<v Examiner>In type 1 diabetes the body fails to make insulin.

10
00:00:28.203 --> 00:00:31.492
<v Examiner>In type 2, the body can't respond to insulin when it's secreted.

11
00:00:31.942 --> 00:00:33.389
Case 6 is two tracks.

12
00:00:33.389 --> 00:00:35.249
This one is insulin itself.

13
00:00:35.699 --> 00:00:36.256
<v Examiner>Two marks.

14
00:00:36.256 --> 00:00:38.984
<v Examiner>Where in the body, specifically, is insulin made?

15
00:00:39.434 --> 00:00:43.001
In the endocrine pancreas, in the islets of Langerhans.

16
00:00:43.001 --> 00:00:48.318
Specifically, the beta cells of the islets make insulin, which lowers blood sugar:

17
00:00:48.318 --> 00:00:52.144
after a meal, it tells the body to store the extra glucose.

18
00:00:52.144 --> 00:00:54.090
The alpha cells make glucagon.

19
00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.527
Her memory trick: glucagon goes with the alpha cells.

20
00:00:57.527 --> 00:00:59.018
Think of the A in both.

21
00:00:59.468 --> 00:01:00.186
<v Examiner>Three marks.

22
00:01:00.186 --> 00:01:05.036
<v Examiner>What hormone, or hormones, act in opposition to insulin, and where are they made?

23
00:01:05.486 --> 00:01:09.694
The hook: glucagon first, then the stress pair, adrenaline and cortisol,

24
00:01:09.694 --> 00:01:11.097
then growth and thyroid.

25
00:01:11.547 --> 00:01:16.001
Glucagon, from the alpha cells of the pancreas, which raises blood glucose.

26
00:01:16.001 --> 00:01:20.515
Adrenaline, also called epinephrine, from the adrenal medulla, and cortisol,

27
00:01:20.515 --> 00:01:21.941
from the adrenal cortex.

28
00:01:21.941 --> 00:01:26.455
Both of those run the stress response, fight or flight and long-term stress,

29
00:01:26.455 --> 00:01:29.068
when the body needs ready access to glucose.

30
00:01:29.068 --> 00:01:33.463
And growth hormone, from the anterior pituitary, and the thyroid hormones,

31
00:01:33.463 --> 00:01:35.185
which are tied to metabolism.

32
00:01:35.185 --> 00:01:37.680
Changes in them can raise blood sugar too.

33
00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:39.807
<v Examiner>Four marks.

34
00:01:39.807 --> 00:01:42.640
<v Examiner>Explain what insulin does at a cellular level.

35
00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:47.258
<v Examiner>Think about how it travels in the body, what it binds to, and what happens.

36
00:01:47.708 --> 00:01:50.908
The hook: travel, bind, move GLUT 4, let glucose in.

37
00:01:51.358 --> 00:01:54.194
Insulin travels through the body in the blood.

38
00:01:54.194 --> 00:01:58.632
It binds to insulin receptors on the surface of insulin-sensitive cells.

39
00:01:58.632 --> 00:02:03.933
Once it's bound, a cascade of events inside the cell moves the GLUT 4 transporter from

40
00:02:03.933 --> 00:02:06.337
inside the cell into the cell membrane.

41
00:02:06.337 --> 00:02:11.761
And in the membrane, GLUT 4 is a transport protein that lets glucose move into the cell,

42
00:02:11.761 --> 00:02:15.337
down its concentration gradient, by facilitated diffusion.

43
00:02:16.787 --> 00:02:17.618
<v Examiner>One mark each.

44
00:02:17.618 --> 00:02:19.282
<v Examiner>Insulin's role in the liver.

45
00:02:19.282 --> 00:02:20.173
<v Examiner>In the muscles.

46
00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:21.480
<v Examiner>And in the fat tissue.

47
00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:26.277
The liver: it stimulates the conversion of glucose to glycogen,

48
00:02:26.277 --> 00:02:29.382
the storage form of glucose, by glycogenesis.

49
00:02:29.382 --> 00:02:35.178
Enzymes join the glucose molecules together into the larger glycogen polysaccharide.

50
00:02:35.628 --> 00:02:37.144
The muscles: the same.

51
00:02:37.144 --> 00:02:39.695
Glucose to glycogen, by glycogenesis.

52
00:02:39.695 --> 00:02:42.452
Her formative test words it another way:

53
00:02:42.452 --> 00:02:48.380
insulin stimulates muscle cells to insert GLUT 4 into the membrane, to let glucose in.

54
00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:52.033
Both are true, so give the one the question is after.

55
00:02:52.483 --> 00:02:57.753
The fat tissue: it stimulates the conversion of glucose to fat, by lipogenesis.

56
00:02:57.753 --> 00:03:02.489
Fat is the most efficient storage form: more energy in a smaller space.

57
00:03:03.939 --> 00:03:07.313
Her formative test has three more blanks on this,

58
00:03:07.313 --> 00:03:10.481
and they're easy marks if the words are ready.

59
00:03:10.481 --> 00:03:14.614
Glycogenesis, glucose to glycogen, is stimulated by insulin.

60
00:03:14.614 --> 00:03:19.159
Glycogenolysis, breaking glycogen down, is stimulated by glucagon.

61
00:03:19.159 --> 00:03:24.531
And gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis together are stimulated by glucagon and

62
00:03:24.531 --> 00:03:25.357
epinephrine.

63
00:03:25.807 --> 00:03:26.409
<v Examiner>Once more.

64
00:03:26.409 --> 00:03:27.493
<v Examiner>What insulin does.

65
00:03:29.943 --> 00:03:35.560
Insulin is made by the beta cells of the islets of Langerhans, in the endocrine pancreas,

66
00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:39.157
and it lowers blood sugar; the alpha cells make glucagon.

67
00:03:39.157 --> 00:03:43.007
Its opponents: glucagon, adrenaline from the adrenal medulla,

68
00:03:43.007 --> 00:03:47.740
cortisol from the adrenal cortex, growth hormone, and the thyroid hormones.

69
00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:51.527
At a cell, insulin travels in the blood, binds its receptor,

70
00:03:51.527 --> 00:03:57.207
and a cascade moves GLUT 4 into the membrane, letting glucose in by facilitated diffusion.

71
00:03:57.207 --> 00:04:01.561
In the liver and muscles it drives glycogenesis, glucose to glycogen.

72
00:04:01.561 --> 00:04:03.265
In fat tissue, lipogenesis.
